                      "questions":  [
                                        {
                                            "q":  "Explain the central idea of \"Everest is not the Only Peak.\"",
                                            "ans":  "The central idea of the poem is that greatness is not limited to the highest or most famous achievement. Everest stands for a great peak, but the poet says every hillock also has a summit. This means every field of life has its own dignity and every person can reach a personal peak through devotion and courage. The poem values the way we live more than the height we reach. It praises people who do not cringe, do not bend for gain, honour competence and merit, and live with dignity. Thus, the poem is a song of self-respect and optimism."
                                        },
                                        {
                                            "q":  "How does the poem celebrate dignity and honour?",
                                            "ans":  "The poem celebrates dignity and honour by showing that true pride comes from the way one lives. The speaker says that whatever we try, we must do with deep and true devotion. We should repel defeat with courage, avoid cringing before others and refuse to seek gain by dishonourable means. The poet praises those who do not stoop and calls them kings. He says honour is common to all and that no one needs to be poor in dignity and pride. The poem therefore teaches that honourable living is greater than outward success."
                                        },
                                        {
                                            "q":  "In what way is every hillock similar to Everest?",
                                            "ans":  "Every hillock is similar to Everest because both have a summit. Everest is famous and high, but even a small hillock has its own top. The poet uses this comparison to explain that every person and every activity can have its own form of excellence. It is not necessary to become the greatest in the world to live meaningfully. A person who works with devotion, courage and self-respect reaches a summit in his or her own field. Thus the poem democratises success and honours every sincere achievement."
                                        },
                                        {
                                            "q":  "The poem does not focus on the destination but the journey towards it. Discuss.",
                                            "ans":  "The poem focuses more on the journey than the destination. The poet says, \"The height you reach is not that we care.\" What matters is how one reaches one\u0027s goal. The journey must be filled with devotion, courage, duty, dignity and honour. The speaker values people who do not stoop, who stand on their own and who respect competence and merit. Everest may be the highest peak, but every hillock has a summit. This means success is not only the final position; it is the honourable effort made on the way."
                                        },
                                        {
                                            "q":  "Write an essay on optimism in the poem.",
                                            "ans":  "The poem is full of optimism because it tells every person that life has a peak worth reaching. It does not limit achievement to Everest or to famous people. Instead, it says every hillock has a summit. The speaker is proud, but not arrogant; humble, but not weak. He believes defeat can be repelled and courage can become a fort. He says honour is common to all, and no one need be poor in dignity and pride. This positive message encourages students to work sincerely and value their own path."
                                        },
                                        {
                                            "q":  "How does the poet describe true success?",
                                            "ans":  "The poet describes true success as honourable living. Success is not merely reaching a great height or gaining fame. It lies in doing every task with deep devotion, refusing defeat, keeping courage as one\u0027s fort, praising the deserving and nourishing those who nourish the world. A successful person does not stoop or cringe for gain. The poet bows before competence and merit, not mere status. He says true people who stand on their own become the ladder for the rise of Man. True success is therefore linked with dignity, merit and service."
                                        },
                                        {
                                            "q":  "How is the poem relevant to modern life?",
                                            "ans":  "The poem is highly relevant to modern life because people often compare themselves with famous achievers and feel small. The poet reminds us that Everest is not the only peak. Every field and every person has a summit. A student, worker, artist, farmer or professional can succeed by doing their duty with devotion, courage and self-respect. The poem also warns against cringing for gain and losing dignity. In a competitive world, it teaches that honour, competence and merit are more important than fame or height. It encourages personal excellence and ethical living."
                                        }
